nodecode-qdrant-memory is a rewrite of cnkang's cnkang/hermes-plugin-qdrant-memory:

  https://github.com/cnkang/hermes-plugin-qdrant-memory
  commit 6ddf7215479692e7882f3065885931f01908c0ac
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Kang Liu (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's user-facing memory — four scoped tools with their
argument schemas (qdrant_memory_search, qdrant_memory_add, qdrant_memory_update,
qdrant_memory_delete), the canonical-text deduplication of an explicit write,
the namespace scoping of every read and write, the prefetch of what the store
holds about the newest ask before the model answers, and the background filing
of a finished turn. The Python (qdrant-client, an embedded Qdrant, an ONNX
embedding model) is Common Lisp here: nlk:http speaks Qdrant's REST API and an
embeddings endpoint, one section declaration types the settings, and four
nlk:define-contract declarations type the tools' arguments so the schema the
model is shown and the body that runs are one shape.

Left out: the parts of the original that are a Python runtime rather than a
memory — its embedded Qdrant and bundled ONNX embedder (this cell speaks to a
Qdrant and an embeddings endpoint the operator already runs), its SQLite
admission ledger and crash-replay, the LLM extraction that distilled a turn
into facts (a cell takes no second model call, so a captured turn is filed as
the operator's own sentence and an explicit add is written as asked), its Mem0
migration and quality-metrics commands, its optional authenticated shared
owner with device grants and an HTTPS MCP bridge, and the standalone CLI and
stdio MCP server, which are other front ends for the same runtime rather than
the plugin's Hermes surface.
